 @burgeal  Mhmm looks like they are dead set on overgimping a lot of the products this generation in different ways. Because there's nothing wrong with the architecture, it's good. It was locked in several years ago, but i don't see a failing here.

You can always just double VRAM capacity, it's not a problem. Easy last minute adjustment for memory capacity pressure.

And then you think of them designing a die that would become a 4060ti and limiting its VRAM bandwidth by a lot compared to what was 3060ti, while increasing shader throughput massively, do you want to say nobody came to think that the result would be unbalanced and very constrained by VRAM throughput? That when they were taping it out one year ago, they couldn't have extended the bus to 160 bit, so 10GB base configuration and 25% more VRAM throughput, at barely any increase cost, mere 16 more memory pins? You can always cut things down later, you can't expand them, that's true, but you'll have to bring out better arguments to convince me that they weren't deliberately overgimping it and knew it. If you have a popular product and you have excess features in the silicon that you aren't using, you just respin for lower cost, it's fine, they have done this numerous times before.

